Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

In period gothic live-action, NARRATOR, gaunt and clean-shaven in his early thirties, with unkempt black hair, a black waistcoat, and a white shirt, sits rigidly at a bare table facing an unseen listener.

(SOUND: a wall clock ticks beneath distant wind)

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

NARRATOR
How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

Morning light leaks through shutters onto MR. VALE, a frail man in his seventies with thin white hair, a hollow face, and a worn dark robe over his nightshirt. NARRATOR sets a tea tray beside him.

(SOUND: porcelain clicks against a saucer; a distant carriage rolls past)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but, once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

MR. VALE raises his face. One pale blue eye, clouded by a filmy veil, catches the light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye!

NARRATOR watches the cloudy eye without blinking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so, by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The gaunt, black-haired NARRATOR turns a dark lantern over in his long fingers. His black waistcoat is neat; his smile is controlled.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge gives a tiny creak)

NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me—how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

NARRATOR
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The shutters are closed against robbers. At midnight, NARRATOR eases the latch and slips his black-haired head through a narrow opening while MR. VALE sleeps in the curtained bed.

(SOUND: a latch lifts; bed linens whisper)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently! When the opening would admit my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out.

Seven midnight visits dissolve into one another. NARRATOR advances by inches, never letting the sleeping man stir.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed.

NARRATOR slowly uncovers the lantern. A single thin ray rests on MR. VALE'S shut eyelid.

(SOUND: the faint metallic complaint of the lantern hinge)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - DAY

At daybreak, NARRATOR, gaunt and dark-haired in the same black waistcoat, brings fresh tea to MR. VALE, whose thin white hair and faded robe remain unchanged. The old man accepts it without suspicion.

(SOUND: morning birds beyond the shutters; a spoon stirs tea)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, and spoke courageously to him, calling him by name in a hearty tone, and inquiring how he had passed the night.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The gaunt NARRATOR leans toward the bare table, black hair fallen over his brow. He holds the closed lantern as if it were a prize.

(SOUND: the wall clock marks midnight)

NARRATOR
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

NARRATOR
I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph. To think that there I was, opening the door, little by little, and he not even to dream of my secret deeds or thoughts.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The chamber is black as pitch, its shutters fastened tight. NARRATOR, gaunt and black-haired in his black waistcoat, pushes the door inward while MR. VALE lies tense beneath the bedclothes.

(SOUND: a suppressed chuckle; a bed rope creaks sharply)

NARRATOR'S thumb slips on the lantern's tin fastening. MR. VALE springs upright, his pale blue eye wide in the darkness.

(SOUND: tin snaps against tin; silence swallows the room)

MR. VALE
Who’s there?

NARRATOR does not move. Time crawls as MR. VALE remains upright, listening into the dark.

(SOUND: death-watch beetles tap inside the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening.

MR. VALE releases a low, stifled groan. His hands clutch the blanket to his throat.

(SOUND: a mortal groan; wind sighs in the chimney)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

MR. VALE'S lips form silent explanations to himself as the darkness seems to press closer.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. He had been trying to fancy it nothing but the wind in the chimney—only a mouse crossing the floor—or merely a cricket which had made a single chirp.

A long bar of black shadow stretches from the door across MR. VALE'S bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him and enveloped the victim.

NARRATOR opens the lantern by the smallest crevice. A dim thread of light shoots across the room and falls full upon the pale blue eye.

(SOUND: the lantern hinge trembles; MR. VALE'S breath catches)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. All a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

NARRATOR holds the ray fixed on the eye. A low, dull, quick beat begins beneath the stillness.

(SOUND: a muffled heartbeat, like a watch enveloped in cotton)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart.

Sweat shines on NARRATOR'S face. He holds the lantern motionless while MR. VALE'S chest jolts beneath the blanket.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows quicker, louder, nearer)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Even yet I refrained and kept still. I scarcely breathed. But the beating grew louder, louder every moment! I thought the heart must burst.

NARRATOR'S eyes dart toward the wall shared with the neighbor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man’s hour had come!

NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the room. MR. VALE shrieks once as NARRATOR drags him to the floor and hauls the heavy bed over him.

(SOUND: a single shriek; the bed crashes down)

The bed shudders. NARRATOR kneels beside it, smiling as the muffled beat persists beneath the weight.

(SOUND: a heartbeat thuds under the bed, then slowly dies)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound. At length it ceased.

NARRATOR removes the bed and presses his hand to MR. VALE'S chest. The pale eye is finally shut.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man was dead. I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Back at the bare table, NARRATOR remains gaunt, black-haired, and formally dressed in his black waistcoat. He shuts the lantern with quiet satisfaction.

(SOUND: the lantern clicks closed)

NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

Gray light gathers behind the shutters. NARRATOR'S black hair clings to his damp brow; his black waistcoat is rumpled over rolled white cuffs. He works methodically beside the covered corpse.

(SOUND: saw teeth scrape wood; a metal tub rings softly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence.

NARRATOR dismembers the corpse, separating the head, arms, and legs. A covered tub waits beneath his work.

NARRATOR pries up three planks from the floor and places the remains between the scantlings. He settles the boards back into place, seamless beneath his hands.

(SOUND: floorboards lift, then settle flush)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong. There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

INT. FRONT HALL - PRE-DAWN

The iron hall clock points to four. NARRATOR crosses the narrow front hall as three uniformed constables wait beyond the street door: OFFICER HALE, broad-shouldered and gray-mustached; OFFICER FENWICK, lean and red-haired; and OFFICER SLOANE, young and solemn.

(SOUND: the clock strikes four; knuckles knock at the street door)

NARRATOR opens the door with a light heart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There entered three men, who introduced themselves, with perfect suavity, as officers of the police. A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night, and they had been deputed to search the premises.

NARRATOR
Gentlemen, welcome. The shriek was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

NARRATOR takes the officers through the house, opening doors and gesturing freely toward every darkened room.

(SOUND: constables' boots cross the hallboards; keys jingle on a belt)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I bade them search—search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

Three chairs stand over the restored floorboards. NARRATOR places his own chair directly above the hidden corpse, while OFFICER HALE, OFFICER FENWICK, and OFFICER SLOANE sit around him.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape the planks; rain begins against the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the enthusiasm of my confidence, I brought chairs into the room and desired them here to rest from their fatigues, while I myself placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse of the victim.

The officers chat pleasantly among themselves. NARRATOR answers with eager ease, then his smile begins to stiffen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

NARRATOR'S face drains of color. He grips the arms of his chair and stares at the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

(SOUND: a faint, dull beating rises beneath the polite conversation)

NARRATOR talks more rapidly, but the officers continue smiling. He suddenly realizes the sound is not within his ears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

NARRATOR rises. He paces in violent strides, arguing over trifles and gesticulating at the officers.

(SOUND: the heartbeat quickens; NARRATOR grates his chair across the boards)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? I paced the floor to and fro with heavy strides, but the noise steadily increased.

The officers continue their pleasant conversation. NARRATOR'S composure collapses into a snarl.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard! They suspected! They knew! They were making a mockery of my horror!

(SOUND: the heartbeat pounds louder than the storm; NARRATOR'S breath breaks)

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The gaunt NARRATOR sits again at the bare table, black waistcoat buttoned, black hair disordered, his fixed smile fading as he waits for the unseen listener.

(SOUND: the wall clock ticks with a faint, relentless double-beat)

FADE OUT.

> THE END <
